---
// Alimi against Vanta and Drata, on the things their own customers complain about. Every statement
// about either vendor is a reported figure or a reviewer's complaint with its source on file in
// docs/research/vanta-drata-complaints.md; nothing here is our own measurement. The page stopped
// listing the sources on Nov 3, 2026 (founder's decision) and keeps one line saying the figures are
// ranges. Alimi has no customers yet, so its column describes how the platform is built, not results.
// Re-check the sources before each quarter's update.
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Callout from "../components/Callout.astro";
import { brand, platform, quote, choosableFrameworks, alwaysIncludedFrameworks, questionnaireAllowances, money } from "../lib/config.js";

const plat = platform();
const silver50 = quote({ tierId: "silver", users: 50 });
const addon50 = quote({ tierId: "silver", users: 50, frameworks: 2 });
const addonYear = addon50.yearly - silver50.yearly;
const catalog = choosableFrameworks();
const posture = alwaysIncludedFrameworks()[0];
const allowances = questionnaireAllowances();
const checked = "October and November 2026";

const themes = [
  {
    id: "renewal",
    title: "The renewal that costs more than the first year",
    reviews: "The most repeated complaint in low-rated reviews of both platforms. Buyers describe first-year discounts of 20 to 70% that are not locked in, renewal quotes 30 to 50% above year one, and contracts with 5 to 10% yearly escalators (UnderDefense on Vanta; about 8% at Drata, negotiable to 3%). One Capterra reviewer was charged a $15,600 renewal without a clear notice; another paid for a two-year contract after stopping use.",
    alimi: `${plat.billing} The price is on this site, per user, and the invoice follows your headcount each month, so there is no quote to negotiate and no cliff in year two.`,
  },
  {
    id: "contract",
    title: "Cancellation and auto-renewal",
    reviews: "Low-rated Capterra and Trustpilot reviews of Vanta describe refused early termination, a 60 to 90 day notice window that auto-renews the contract if missed, and a customer who lost access to an auto-renewed year they had paid for. Drata's terms are reported as annual, paid up front, renewing unless cancelled 30 to 60 days ahead.",
    alimi: `A 12-month term paid monthly, or month to month for ${Math.round(plat.billingTerms.monthly_premium * 100)}% more that ends at any month's end. Renewal is a conversation, not a clause: nothing in the contract raises the price on its own.`,
  },
  {
    id: "addons",
    title: "A framework, a trust page and a vendor list each cost extra",
    reviews: "Reported add-on prices: each additional framework about $5,000 a year at Vanta and $3,000 to $10,000 at Drata; vendor risk management $11,200 to $13,600 a year; a trust page $6,000 to $10,000; questionnaire automation $10,000 to $16,000; access reviews $5,000 to $12,000. A Capterra reviewer calls it being charged for features that should be standard, and a G2 reviewer says modules must be bought separately and that this was 'not mentioned during initial marketing and pre-sale phase'.",
    alimi: `Vendors, risks, questionnaires and the trust page are inside the ${plat.tiers[1].name} tier at a per-user price, not modules. A second framework is ${money(plat.frameworks.addon)} per user a month: ${money(addonYear)} a year for a 50-person company, with every added framework discounted from the fifth. The whole price list is on this site, add-ons included; there is no module to discover after the sale.`,
  },
  {
    id: "support",
    title: "Small customers get the sales call, then the queue",
    reviews: "Trustpilot reviewers of Vanta (3.1 out of 5 overall) describe aggressive sales outreach followed by no onboarding, generic support replies and no human response to a cancellation; Gartner Peer Insights reviewers name limited support and cost as the reasons they consider switching. A Drata reviewer on Gartner reports far more internal security, IT and policy effort than expected.",
    alimi: `${brand.display_name} is built for companies of 10 people and up that cannot hire a security team. A named compliance lead is in every tier, with a quarterly call on ${plat.tiers[0].name} and a monthly one from ${plat.tiers[1].name}; the founder answers the support address.`,
  },
  {
    id: "noise",
    title: "Noisy tests and false positives",
    reviews: "Capterra reviewers of Vanta report alarm fatigue from frequent failing-test notifications and too many alerts for minor issues. In a set of 23 Drata user interviews, a power user scoring it 2 out of 5 cites \"too many false positives, intermittent integrations and workflow quirks for what we're paying\"; others say finding the resource behind a failed test is painful and that \"a green dashboard does not mean a control is well designed\".",
    alimi: "Every test shows the raw evidence it read and the rule it applied, so you can see why it failed. A false positive gets a note with a reason that stays in the audit log, and the checks are the ones an insurer or an auditor asks about, not every setting a cloud exposes.",
  },
  {
    id: "integrations",
    title: "Integrations that quietly stop syncing",
    reviews: "A healthcare security officer in the Drata interview set: \"when one quietly stops syncing, confidence in the dashboard drops fast.\" G2 and Capterra reviewers of both products ask for deeper integrations and report that custom setups need manual work; a 2024 Reddit thread lists connector and multi-framework frustrations.",
    alimi: "Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace done properly first, with a data-quality page that shows when each connection last read your tenant and what it could not read. A silent gap is shown as an error naming the permission, never as a pass.",
  },
  {
    id: "agent",
    title: "An agent on every laptop",
    reviews: "Capterra reviewers of Vanta say the required endpoint agent is hard to roll out to remote teams and object to it not being open source; reports of high CPU use on older machines and of staff worrying the agent watches them appear in review summaries. Drata's documentation has the agent override the device-management tool when both report a device.",
    alimi: "No agent. Device compliance comes from what you already run, Intune or Google endpoint management, read through the same read-only connection. Nothing is installed on anyone's computer.",
  },
  {
    id: "templates",
    title: "Generic policies and a product written for auditors",
    reviews: "Capterra reviewers of Vanta say the policy templates need updating and that the policy generator loses its automation once a document is edited outside it. Interviewed Drata users warn that compliance looks \"deceptively simple during a demo\" and that someone still has to make the scope and remediation decisions.",
    alimi: `${brand.display_name}'s policies are written for a company of your size and tailored with you in a workshop; versions, approvals and sign-offs live in the platform. The scoping and policy calls that software leaves to you are made with a person, in plain English, with every term explained the first time.`,
  },
  {
    id: "audit",
    title: "The bundled audit, and who the auditor works for",
    reviews: "A Trustpilot reviewer of Drata (2.6 of 5 from four reviews) says the bundled audit 'went horrible', that switching auditors took many weeks and that a deal was lost in the meantime; a Capterra reviewer of Vanta paid for the platform and 'still had to handle auditor ourselves'. Some r/soc2 users allege that auditor partnerships favour volume over thoroughness; those are allegations, not findings.",
    alimi: `The CPA firm is your choice and is paid by you; ${brand.display_name} takes no fee from any auditor, and the export pack goes to whichever firm you pick. On ${plat.tiers[3].name}, ${brand.display_name} runs the audit with the firm for you, and the firm stays independent.`,
  },
  {
    id: "priorities",
    title: "A green dashboard that does not say what to do today",
    reviews: "G2 reviewers of Vanta (syndicated on AWS Marketplace, September 2026) ask for a filter for the controls that need attention now, report stale test statuses, and want change tracking and rollback; one says the AI returns a blank for 'no evidence found' without saying whether it searched. PeerSpot reviewers of Drata (4.0 of 5 from ten) say readiness indicators can be misleading, and an interviewed GRC consultant notes that 'a green dashboard does not mean a control is well designed'.",
    alimi: `The home screen is a short list in order of insurer impact: the top five fixes and what is due in the next 30 days. Two scores, audit readiness and the ${posture.name}, show passing and being secure apart. Every status is computed, never typed, a fixed check can be re-run on the spot, policies and controls keep a version history, and the assistant says what it searched when it finds nothing.`,
  },
  {
    id: "expertise",
    title: "Built for a company that already has a security person",
    reviews: "SoftwareAdvice and Capterra reviewers of Vanta at 2 to 50 person companies say the product seems built for enterprise clients, that the pricing is too high for them, and ask for more automation and help with remediation because they have no IT team; PeerSpot reviewers of Drata say setting up integrations needs specialist knowledge, and a Trustpilot reviewer warns small businesses off a contract that 'will add no real value'. Interviewed Drata users say someone still has to own scope, fixes and approval decisions.",
    alimi: `${brand.display_name} is priced per user from ${plat.tiers[0].perUser} a month and built for companies of 10 people and up that cannot hire a security team. A named compliance lead in every tier owns the scoping and policy calls, every failing check carries a plain-English fix, and the fixed-fee services do the remediation when there is nobody to hand it to.`,
  },
];

const facts = [
  { label: "Price published", vanta: "No; quote by sales call", drata: "No; quote by sales call", alimi: `Yes, per user a month, on this site` },
  { label: "Entry plan, 50 people, one framework", vanta: "$7,500 to $12,000 a year reported (Essentials); $14,000 for 1 to 20 people on AWS Marketplace", drata: "$15,000 a year for 1 to 50 people (Foundation, AWS Marketplace); $7,500 to $15,000 reported", alimi: `${money(silver50.yearly)} a year (${plat.tiers[0].name}, ${silver50.perUserText})` },
  { label: "Each additional framework", vanta: "About $5,000 a year reported", drata: "$3,000 to $10,000 a year reported", alimi: `${money(plat.frameworks.addon)} per user a month; ${money(addonYear)} a year at 50 people` },
  { label: "Renewal", vanta: "5 to 10% yearly escalators and 30 to 50% year-two increases reported", drata: "About 8% escalator; 10 to 30% increases reported", alimi: plat.billingTerms.price_lock_months > plat.billingTerms.term_months ? `No automatic increase; price locked for ${plat.billingTerms.price_lock_months} months` : "No automatic increase; any change at renewal is agreed with you, never applied by a clause" },
  { label: "Term", vanta: "Annual, paid up front; auto-renews unless cancelled 60 to 90 days ahead", drata: "Annual, paid up front; renews unless cancelled 30 to 60 days ahead", alimi: "12-month term paid monthly, or month to month" },
  { label: "Device checks", vanta: "Agent on each computer", drata: "Agent on each computer", alimi: "No agent on any computer: read from Intune or Google endpoint management once those connections ship (planned, not connected yet)" },
  { label: "Who makes the scoping and policy calls", vanta: "You, or a paid implementation partner", drata: "You, or a paid implementation partner", alimi: "A named compliance lead, in every tier" },
  { label: "Frameworks", vanta: "35 and more", drata: "About 30 pre-mapped", alimi: `${catalog.length} in the catalog, one included, the rest add-ons; plus the ${posture.name} in every plan` },
  { label: "Questionnaires included", vanta: "25 a year on Plus, 144 on Professional; more as an add-on", drata: "100 with the Trust Center Foundation tier", alimi: allowances.charAt(0).toUpperCase() + allowances.slice(1) },
  { label: "Outcome numbers on the website", vanta: "'2,000 hrs. saved annually', '20% faster deal cycles', not tied to a named customer", drata: "'3x productivity', '$20M annual revenue accelerated', no method given", alimi: "None. No outcome number appears until a named customer has agreed to it" },
  { label: "Integrations", vanta: "300 and more", drata: "200 and more", alimi: "Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace today; the next ones are listed on the integrations page" },
  { label: "Reviews", vanta: "G2 4.6 of 5; Capterra and SoftwareAdvice 4.2 (33); Trustpilot 3.1 (33)", drata: "G2 4.7 of 5 (1,399); Capterra 4.8 (6); Gartner 4.1 (17); PeerSpot 4.0 (10); Trustpilot 2.6 (4)", alimi: "None yet; pilots open in January 2027" },
];

const stronger = [
  "Hundreds of integrations each, where Alimi starts with Microsoft 365 and Google Workspace. A company running AWS, GitHub, Okta and a dozen SaaS tools gets more automated evidence from either of them today.",
  "Years of audits behind them, large auditor networks and a SOC 2 report of their own. Alimi is a company in formation with no customers yet.",
  "Enterprise features Alimi does not plan to build: multiple business units, hundreds of custom tests, SCIM provisioning at scale.",
];

---

<Base title="Compare" description={`Alimi compared with Vanta and Drata on what their own customers complain about in reviews rated below four stars: renewal increases, auto-renewal, add-on fees, support for small customers, noisy tests, silent integrations, laptop agents, bundled audits, unclear priorities and products built for larger teams.`} path="/compare">
  <Hero eyebrow="Compare" title="Alimi, Vanta and Drata, on what their customers complain about">
    <p class="lede">Vanta and Drata are good products for companies with a compliance owner and a budget to match; most of their reviews are four and five stars. We read the rest, the reviews rated below four, and the pricing reports behind them, and built {brand.display_name} around what those customers wanted changed. Every figure here is a reported one, kept on file with its source.</p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/pricing">Alimi's prices</a>
      <a class="button button--secondary" href="#facts">The side-by-side table</a>
    </div>
  </Hero>

  <Section id="complaints" tone="alt" title={`${themes.length} complaints, and what Alimi does about each`} lede={`From Capterra, SoftwareAdvice, Trustpilot, G2, Gartner Peer Insights, PeerSpot, Reddit and a published set of Drata user interviews, read in ${checked}.`}>
    <div class="compare-grid">
      {themes.map((t) => (
        <article class="card" id={t.id}>
          <h3>{t.title}</h3>
          <p><strong>What reviewers say.</strong> {t.reviews}</p>
          <p><strong>What {brand.display_name} does.</strong> {t.alimi}</p>
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="facts" title="Side by side" lede="Vendor figures are as reported in public reviews and third-party pricing guides; neither vendor publishes a price list. Alimi's are from its own published price list.">
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table class="table table--compact">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col"></th><th scope="col">Vanta</th><th scope="col">Drata</th><th scope="col">{brand.display_name}</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {facts.map((f) => (
            <tr>
              <th scope="row">{f.label}</th>
              <td>{f.vanta}</td>
              <td>{f.drata}</td>
              <td>{f.alimi}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Callout label="Why there are no success numbers on this site" tone="brand">
      <p>Both vendors' home pages carry outcome statistics with no customer or method behind them. {brand.display_name}'s rule is the opposite: no number about results until it comes from a named customer who agreed to it. That is also why this page quotes reviewers rather than its own measurements.</p>
    </Callout>
    <p class="muted small" style="margin-top:1rem">Vendor figures are ranges from public reviews and third-party pricing guides read in {checked}, not quotes. Vanta and Drata are trademarks of their owners; {brand.display_name} is not affiliated with either.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="stronger" tone="alt" title="Where Vanta and Drata are stronger" lede="A fair comparison names these too.">
    <ul class="checklist">
      {stronger.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
    </ul>
    <p class="measure">If your company runs a dozen cloud systems or needs an audit next month, one of them is probably the right choice. If you have 10 people or more, one insurer and one or two customers asking, and nobody whose job this is, {brand.display_name} was built for you.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="next" tone="dark" title="See the price for your company" lede="Per user a month, with one framework included and the whole policy library.">
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/pricing#calculator">Work out your price</a>
      <a class="button button--secondary" href="/platform">How the platform works</a>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Base>
